 0.007965087890625, -0.01457977294921875, 0.0158233642578125, 0.03826904296875, -0.0013284683227539062, 0.004421234130859375, 0.0155029296875, 0.0207366943359375, -0.0024623870849609375, -0.00959014892578125, -0.037628173828125, -0.004306793212890625, -0.00921630859375, -0.006427764892578125, 0.0012216567993164062, 0.0023136138916015625, 0.000644683837890625, -0.00556182861328125, 0.007534027099609375, 0.0002624988555908203, -0.02374267578125, 0.00785064697265625, 0.019744873046875, -0.0191650390625, -0.021453857421875, -0.0027141571044921875, -0.01116180419921875, -0.0213165283203125, 0.0108642578125, -0.004985809326171875, -0.013397216796875, 0.019073486328125, -0.020263671875, -0.0070648193359375, -0.01313018798828125, -0.00839996337890625, 0.0189361572265625, 0.00799560546875, 0.02850341796875, -0.018035888671875, 0.006473541259765625, -0.02423095703125, -0.00014960765838623047, 0.003662109375, 0.004108428955078125, -0.0175933837890625, -0.01317596435546875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Permanental processes are a class of stochastic point process models that have been used in spatial statistics and machine learning, particularly for modeling repulsion between points (McCullagh & M\u00f8ller, 2006). However, standard permanental process models typically do not accommodate fixed effects, limiting their ability to incorporate known covariate influences or domain knowledge. In this work, we address this limitation by extending the permanental process model to include fixed effects, thereby enabling interpretable modeling of covariate information alongside nonparametric spatial structure.\n\nLet \\( \\mathcal{X} \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^d \\) denote the domain of interest (e.g., a spatial region), and let \\( k: \\mathcal{X} \\times \\mathcal{X} \\to \\mathbb{R} \\) be a positive definite kernel. The associated Reproducing Kernel Hilbert Space (RKHS), \\( \\mathcal{H}_k \\), is the space of functions on \\( \\mathcal{X} \\) with inner product \\( \\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle_{\\mathcal{H}_k} \\) and norm \\( \\|f\\|_{\\mathcal{H}_k} = \\sqrt{\\langle f, f \\rangle_{\\mathcal{H}_k}} \\). We denote by \\( z(x) \\in \\mathbb{R}^p \\) a vector of known covariates at location \\( x \\).\n\nOur main contribution is to formulate a permanental process model for a point process on \\( \\mathcal{X} \\) with an intensity function of the form\n\\[\n\\lambda(x) = \\exp\\left( \\beta^\\top z(x) + f(x) \\right),\n\\]\nwhere \\( \\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}^p \\) is a vector of fixed effect coefficients, and \\( f \\in \\mathcal{H}_k \\) is a function in the RKHS. This extends the standard permanental process, which typically models \\( \\lambda(x) \\) as a function in an RKHS without explicit fixed effects. By including \\( \\beta^\\top z(x) \\), our model allows for direct incorporation of interpretable covariate effects, distinguishing it from prior work such as McCullagh & M\u00f8ller (2006), where such fixed effects are not present.\n\n**Identifiability and Theoretical Guarantees:**  \nIntroducing fixed effects into permanental processes raises statistical challenges, particularly identifiability. If the covariates \\( z(x) \\) are highly correlated with functions in the RKHS (i.e., if there exists \\( g \\in \\mathcal{H}_k \\) such that \\( g(x) \\approx \\beta^\\top z(x) \\)), then the decomposition of \\( \\lambda(x) \\) into fixed and nonparametric components is not unique. Sufficient conditions for identifiability include: (i) the covariates \\( z(x) \\) are linearly independent of the RKHS (i.e., no nontrivial linear combination of \\( z(x) \\) lies in \\( \\mathcal{H}_k \\)), and (ii) the kernel \\( k \\) is chosen so that its RKHS does not contain the span of \\( z(x) \\). In practice, regularization (finite \\( \\sigma^2 \\)) mitigates identifiability issues. We provide a simulation-based investigation in the supplement, showing that identifiability failures manifest as instability in the estimated \\( \\beta \\) and \\( f \\) when covariates are collinear with kernel features or when \\( \\sigma^2 \\) is very large.\n\n**Prior Specification and Sensitivity:**  \nWe place a Gaussian prior on \\( f \\) (zero mean, covariance operator induced by \\( k \\)), and a Gaussian prior with variance \\( \\sigma^2 \\) on \\( \\beta \\). The choice of \\( \\sigma^2 \\) is critical: a very large (diffuse) prior allows the data to determine the fixed effects but can lead to identifiability problems and overfitting, especially when covariates are weakly informative or collinear with the kernel features. We recommend selecting \\( \\sigma^2 \\) via cross-validation (e.g., maximizing held-out log-likelihood) or empirical Bayes (maximizing marginal likelihood). In our experiments, we vary \\( \\sigma^2 \\) over a grid and report predictive performance and parameter stability; see Figure S1 in the supplement for sensitivity analysis. We find that moderate regularization (\\( \\sigma^2 \\) chosen by cross-validation) improves predictive performance and interpretability compared to the diffuse prior limit.\n\n**Inference and Computational Methods:**  \nFor inference, we use penalized likelihood estimation. The log-likelihood for observed points \\( \\{x_i\\}_{i=1}^n \\) is\n\\[\n\\ell(\\beta, f) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left[ \\beta^\\top z(x_i) + f(x_i) \\right] - \\int_{\\mathcal{X}} \\exp\\left( \\beta^\\top z(x) + f(x) \\right) dx.\n\\]\nWe maximize the penalized log-likelihood\n\\[\n\\ell(\\beta, f) - \\frac{1}{2} \\|f\\|^2_{\\mathcal{H}_k} - \\frac{1}{2\\sigma^2} \\|\\beta\\|^2,\n\\]\nwhere \\( \\|f\\|_{\\mathcal{H}_k} \\) is the RKHS norm and \\( \\sigma^2 \\) is the prior variance for \\( \\beta \\). In the diffuse prior limit (\\( \\sigma^2 \\to \\infty \\)), the penalty on \\( \\beta \\) vanishes. The representer theorem (Wahba, 1990, Theorem 1.1) ensures that the solution for \\( f \\) lies in the span of kernel functions evaluated at the observed data points, i.e., \\( f(x) = \\sum_{j=1}^n \\alpha_j k(x, x_j) \\), reducing the problem to finite dimensions.\n\nWe optimize the penalized likelihood using block coordinate descent:  \n1. **Initialization:** We initialize \\( \\beta \\) via logistic regression on the covariates, and \\( f \\) as zero.  \n2. **Update \\( f \\):** With \\( \\beta \\) fixed, we solve for \\( f \\) by optimizing the penalized likelihood over the coefficients \\( \\alpha \\) using L-BFGS or conjugate gradient methods, with convergence assessed by relative change in objective (< \\(10^{-6}\\)).  \n3. **Update \\( \\beta \\):** With \\( f \\) fixed, we update \\( \\beta \\) using gradient-based optimization (e.g., L-BFGS), again with convergence assessed by relative change in objective.  \n4. **Repeat** until convergence (typically < 20 iterations).  \n\nThe integral over \\( \\mathcal{X} \\) is approximated using adaptive quadrature for low dimensions (\\( d \\leq 3 \\)), or Monte Carlo integration for higher dimensions. For large datasets, we employ sparse kernel approximations (e.g., inducing points) and stochastic optimization (mini-batch updates) to improve scalability. The computational complexity per iteration is \\( O(n^2) \\) for dense kernels, reduced to \\( O(nm) \\) with \\( m \\ll n \\) inducing points.\n\n**Model Diagnostics and Selection:**  \nWe recommend the following procedures:\n- **Residual analysis:** Compute Pearson or deviance residuals at quadrature points or a grid over \\( \\mathcal{X} \\), and plot spatial patterns to detect lack of fit.\n- **Cross-validation:** Partition data into training and validation sets; select \\( \\sigma^2 \\), kernel parameters, and covariates by maximizing held-out log-likelihood.\n- **Kernel/covariate selection:** Use forward selection or penalized likelihood (e.g., group lasso) to select relevant covariates and kernel hyperparameters.\n- **Information criteria:** Compute AIC or BIC for model comparison.\nIllustrative results and code for these procedures are provided in the supplement.\n\n**Empirical Validation:**  \nWe evaluate our method on both synthetic and real datasets. On synthetic data (points on \\([0,1]^2\\) with a linear covariate and spatially varying component), our method accurately recovers the true fixed effect coefficient and captures the residual spatial structure; omitting the fixed effect leads to biased estimates and poor predictive performance. On a real ecological dataset (see supplement), we compare our method to standard permanental processes and semiparametric Cox processes. Our model achieves improved predictive log-likelihood and yields interpretable fixed effect estimates, while the standard permanental process confounds covariate and spatial effects.\n\n**Related Work:**  \nOur approach is related to semiparametric models (Wahba, 1990; Wood, 2017), which combine parametric and nonparametric components, and to semiparametric Cox processes (Baddeley et al., 2015; Yuan et al., 2017). Unlike McCullagh & M\u00f8ller (2006), who model intensity solely via an RKHS function, our model separates interpretable covariate effects from residual structure. Recent advances in scalable inference for point processes (e.g., sparse Gaussian processes, variational inference; Lloyd et al., 2015; Adams et al., 2009) inform our computational approach. See supplement for a detailed literature review and discussion of novelty.\n\n**Scope, Limitations, and Practical Guidance:**  \nOur model assumes that covariates are correctly specified and that the kernel is appropriate for the"}}